/// Optional register and memory state captured at the time of a fault-class signal.
///
/// Included in signal messages for crash-class signals (e.g. SIGSEGV, SIGBUS)
/// to provide rich post-mortem context without async-signal-safe constraints.
kind thingos.signal.fault = struct {
  /// Address that caused the fault (e.g. faulting memory address for SIGSEGV).
  fault_addr: u64,
  /// Saved instruction pointer at the time of the fault.
  rip: u64,
  /// Saved stack pointer at the time of the fault.
  rsp: u64,
}

/// Typed signal notification — canonical inbox message payload for signal delivery.
///
/// Replaces async-signal-handler interrupt semantics with inbox-safe typed events.
/// Applications receive a `thingos.signal` message in their main event loop
/// instead of having a signal handler interrupted into the execution context.
///
/// The `signum` field carries the POSIX signal number for compatibility.
/// Crash-class signals (e.g. SIGSEGV) populate the `fault` field with rich
/// diagnostic context including the faulting address and register state.
kind thingos.signal = struct {
  /// POSIX signal number (1–63). Preserves compatibility with UNIX signal numbers.
  signum: u8,
  /// Thread ID of the sender, or 0 for kernel-originated signals.
  sender_tid: u64,
  /// Optional fault context for crash-class signals (SIGSEGV, SIGBUS, etc.).
  fault: option<thingos.signal.fault>,
}
